# Scoped intentionally narrow. qontinui-schemas has core.autocrlf=true and no
# other .gitattributes, so a repo-wide `* text=auto eol=lf` would rewrite the
# line endings of ~85% of the tracked tree on the next checkout. Only the
# generated bindings are pinned here.
#
# Two reasons they need pinning:
#
# 1. Without it a long-lived Windows checkout can report the whole generated
#    tree as MODIFIED while the files are byte-identical to HEAD. Observed
#    2026-08-27 on the operator box: 541 files under ts/src/generated reported
#    changed, `git diff` empty, `cmp` against the committed blob matching. That
#    is a stale stat-cache against LF-committed blobs, and it hides real drift
#    inside permanent noise. (A fresh clone does not show it; the repo only
#    grows into the state.)
#
# 2. .github/workflows/schema-drift.yml fails CI when these two trees drift from
#    what codegen produces, and uploads exactly these paths as the
#    `regenerated-bindings` artifact. eol=lf keeps blob == working tree on every
#    platform, so a Windows checkout can never introduce CR and trip that check
#    for a reason unrelated to schema content.
#
# The committed blobs are already LF, so this changes no committed bytes:
# applied to main, `git status` reports 0 modified files and
# `git add --renormalize` on both trees stages 0 changes. It does mean the next
# checkout rewrites ~1090 generated files from CRLF to LF in the WORKING TREE.
ts/src/generated/** text eol=lf
src/qontinui_schemas/generated/** text eol=lf

# Recorded UI Bridge SDK wire fixtures (rust/tests/ui_bridge_sdk_wire.rs). Their
# .provenance.json sidecars pin each fixture's sha256, so a checkout must never
# rewrite their bytes: under core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout would convert
# them to CRLF and fail the digest test locally. `-text` stores and checks them
# out byte-for-byte; the committed blobs are LF already.
rust/tests/fixtures/ui_bridge_sdk/*.json -text

# The glossary source and the Rust table generated from it
# (rust/tests/glossary_source.rs). The test normalises CRLF before hashing and
# comparing, so this is belt and braces: it keeps blob == working tree on every
# platform so the generated file never reads as modified for a line-ending
# reason alone.
glossary/terms.toml text eol=lf
glossary/versions.lock text eol=lf
rust/src/glossary/generated.rs text eol=lf

# qontinui-ci import / gen-workflow golden tests (ci-exec/tests/import_golden.rs).
# The goldens are compared byte-for-byte against the importer's LF output, and
# the fixtures are copies of other repos' origin/main blobs (LF). Under
# core.autocrlf=true a Windows checkout rewrote the goldens to CRLF, so
# ci-exec-windows failed with every line equal ("expected None, got None").
# The committed blobs are already LF; this changes no committed bytes.
ci-exec/tests/golden/** text eol=lf
ci-exec/tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The BoundedReadMeta cross-language fixtures (rust/tests/round_trip.rs,
# tests/test_round_trip.py). Both suites compare against the fixture's exact
# RFC 8785 JCS bytes, so a CRLF checkout under core.autocrlf=true fails the
# Rust half on every Windows box for a reason unrelated to the contract.
# `-text` checks them out byte-for-byte; the committed blobs are LF already.
tests/fixtures/bounded_read_meta_*.json -text
